7. Causal Resolution

Questions such as:

Who caused X?

often fail because they omit causal resolution.

A causal event may contain:

intent
↓
authority
↓
intelligence
↓
planning
↓
logistics
↓
command
↓
execution
↓
proximal action
↓
effect

Different actors may truthfully occupy different causal positions.

Therefore distinguish:

causal contribution
causal proximity
decision authority
execution responsibility
historical credit
moral responsibility

Core distinction:

Effect does not require knowledge of provenance.
Understanding does.
Credit especially does.

Pantheon should preserve the causal graph rather than flattening all participants into one binary answer.

