10. Post-Hoc Semantic Ancestors

AI-scale semantic search changes historical attribution.

Given almost any modern idea, sufficiently large retrieval systems may discover earlier artifacts with partial resemblance.

Therefore similarity alone cannot establish descent.

Distinguish:

PARENT
direct or plausible inheritance
COUSIN
independent convergence around similar structure
SOIL
older material that made a conceptual environment possible

A citation proves historical precedence of the cited artifact.

It does not automatically prove causal inheritance.

Strong ancestry requires

chronology
+
specific similarity
+
plausible exposure
+
transmission path
+
transformation evidence

Core law:

Citation can establish precedence.
Descent requires a plausible inheritance path.

Future novelty analysis should emphasize:

* operationalization;
* integration;
* demonstrated mechanism;
* chronology;
* implementation;
* transmission;
* causal lineage.

Not merely lexical resemblance.

